      "content": "If HMRC suspects undisclosed assets, they won't necessarily audit your entire estate immediately but will likely issue a formal inquiry requesting full particulars and detailed valuations of all holdings. If you provide inaccurate information or fail to respond, they can then issue a Significant Errors of Fact notice, which allows them to reassess the tax charge on the full value of the omitted assets plus hefty penalties. In complex cross-border cases involving foreign trusts, HMRC often relies on third-party data from banks or solicitors in those countries to verify what is actually held outside the UK.",
